---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/*
  Figures from the path-of-leash-resistance repository, counted on
  21 November 2026. Update them when the page is next revised.
*/
const stats = [
  { value: '1,982', label: 'commits since the first one in July 2026' },
  { value: '467', label: 'pull requests merged' },
  { value: '265', label: 'test files guarding the game' },
  { value: '42', label: 'hand-built walks in and around Barcelona' },
];
---

<Base
  title="How I build"
  description="How I ship whole products on my own: written briefs, coding agents working in parallel, and a review gate that nothing skips."
>
  <section class="wrap head">
    <span class="eyebrow">How I build</span>
    <h1>I direct, the agents type.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I build my projects the way a small studio works. I write the plan and the briefs, coding agents
      build in parallel, each on its own branch, and I review, play and test everything before it
      merges. The judgment stays mine; the typing is shared.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-label="Path of Leash Resistance in numbers">
    <ul class="stats">
      {
        stats.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{s.value}</strong>
            <span>{s.label}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Path of Leash Resistance, as of November 2026.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>1. Write it down first</h2>
      <p>
        Every project has a <code>PROJECT.md</code> with its design pillars and roadmap, and an
        <code>AGENTS.md</code> that any coding agent reads before it touches anything: the tech stack,
        the conventions, the game's voice, and the rules that are not up for negotiation. When an
        agent gets something wrong, the fix goes into those files as well as the code, so the
        next agent doesn't repeat it.
      </p>

      <h2>2. One brief per agent, one worktree per brief</h2>
      <p>
        Work goes out in batches. Each piece gets a written brief: a shared preamble with the house
        rules, a shared brief for that kind of work (say, building a new walk), then the specifics.
        Each agent works in its own git worktree on its own branch, so a batch of four new walks, or
        a polish pass across every district of the city, can be built at the same time without
        anyone stepping on anyone else.
      </p>
      <p>
        The briefs carry lessons forward. A batch prompt starts with what earlier batches got wrong:
        a test that assumed its walk was the last one, poles that all grew street lamps. Those
        mistakes don't come back.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Nothing merges unchecked</h2>
      <p>Every pull request has to show its work before it can land:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The walk's own tests and the full suite pass, plus a headless smoke run of the game.</li>
        <li>
          A scripted playtest walks the level from start to finish and logs anyone stuck or inside a
          wall.
        </li>
        <li>Screenshots of every part of the route, in every weather and at night.</li>
        <li>
          A <em>feel card</em>: three to five things to try by hand and what should happen. I play
          them.
        </li>
        <li>A changelog entry, checked by a script.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4. Release like a team would</h2>
      <p>
        A version tag builds the web and Windows versions in CI, pushes them to itch.io, and packages
        the Microsoft Store build. stemma ships the same way, with a pinned release lock so CI tests
        exactly what users get.
      </p>

      <h2>Why work this way</h2>
      <p>
        The hard part of software was never typing. It's deciding what to build, noticing what's
        wrong, and keeping a large codebase coherent as it grows. Agents make the typing cheap, which
        makes those skills matter more, not less. This is how one person ships a game with 50 levels,
        nine chases and four bosses in a few months, alongside a day job.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 18px;
    padding-block: clamp(32px, 6vw, 64px) 48px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(48px, 7vw, 88px);
  }
  .lede {
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 20px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 220px), 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .stats li {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 28px;
    background: var(--feature-bg);
    color: var(--feature-ink);
    border-radius: var(--radius-l);
  }
  .stats strong {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(40px, 5vw, 56px);
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--saffron);
  }
  .stats span {
    color: var(--feature-ink-2);
  }
  .note {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
